as we've been hearing more and more  company executives are joining the  argument now about the referendum today  more than a hundred business leaders  added their names to a declaration in  favor of independence one of those was  frank mckergan a former  global head of equities at rbs the royal  bank of scotland thank you for joining  us  mr mckergan  why have you decided to support  independence when so many other  business leaders have decided that  independence would be bad for the  scottish economy well i think it's wrong  to suggest that there's a whole bunch of  business people on the north side and  only one or two on the yes side through  the campaign i've met literally hundreds  of business people who are convinced by  the yes argument and again and can see  beyond the short-term issues that the no  campaign are throwing out and are much  more excited by the longer term business  opportunities for scots in scotland  what about standard today uh suggesting  that if independence comes they are  going to shift out of scotland and into  england i think what they're talking  about is contingency plans so what  they're saying is that um they are  putting plans together in case a whole  bunch of negative scenarios come about  but i don't see those scenarios coming  about i think there will be a currency  union and there will be a single market  for financial products in the united  kingdom because it's in the best  interest of all parts of what is  currently the united kingdom for that to  be so but i can understand company  executives looking at all sorts of  scenarios that i think are frankly  unlikely and coming up with contingency  plans for that this is nothing new from  standard life they've said this before  it's continuously planning for a  scenario that i don't see coming about  but what you're doing is pinning your  hopes on saying i know better than the  governor of the bank of england i know  better than the chance of the exchequer  i know better  than ed balls all of whom have made it  very clear  that they do not see a currency union  coming about after independence for the  obvious reason that the remaining  members of the united kingdom want to  control their currency and if they had a  currency union with an independent  nation they wouldn't have that control i  think to be fair to the westminster  politicians it seems like they've only  started thinking about these issues in  the last couple of weeks that the  campaign has got incredibly well to be  fair to the governor of the bank of  england he made that speech right back  at the beginning of the year but i don't  expect in the result in the result of a  yes vote i don't expect the rest of the  uk to do scotland any favors but i do  expect them to behave with rational  self-interest i think in the aftermath  of a yes vote if everyone sits down and  looks at the situation say what's in the  best interests of both parties to me  it's absolutely blindingly clear that  that is a currency union with the single  market for financial services  but it also brightly clear isn't it that  were you to be right were there to be a  currency union the sort of financial  disciplines which are being required  currently in all of the uk and would  still be required in the rest of the uk  would mean that alex salmon couldn't  possibly deliver on the economic  promises he's making on welfare and  other things well not surprisingly i  strongly disagree with that i think um  the the plans have been put forward in  the s campaign are eminently achievable  but if what you're saying is that  smoking we're not with the currency  union when you've signed away that  control  i think a currency is the best interest  of both parties and i think scotland can  prosper within that and the same as many  countries have prospered within currency  unions and for people like paul krugman  to come up with scarce stories about um  what happened in the eurozone i think is  is just totally unrealistic that is not  what would happen in a currency union  between scotland and the rest of the uk  why  because one one of the reasons why you  had problems in the eurozone would not  be replicated in a currency union in the  uk you had  countries in the eurozone at completely  different levels of productivity  completely different stages of economic  development scotland and the rest of the  uk have almost identical levels of  productivity and in very similar  economic stages of development as two  very imminent economies who are part of  the uh the commission uh professors  merlies and professor stiglitz pointed  out that's an eminently stable and  suitable um monetary environment for  scotland to prosper within  but why if the scots people decide to  vote for independence  should the rest of the uk  continue to underwrite the scottish  economy it doesn't seem to make sense if  you want to go your own way go your own  way but don't expect other people to  pick up the bill but i don't see it as  the rest of the uk picking up the bill  for scotland i think scotland wants to  sustain underwriting the scottish  economy  being the lender of last resort would be  doing exactly that wouldn't it well for  an independent scotland the financial  sector would be smaller as a percentage  of gdp than it is in the uk currently so  i don't see that being a problem  yes but it would still be the situation  that uh given important companies like  rbs which he used to work for based in  scotland that if there was a currency  union that would mean that voters in  england voters in wales voters in  northern ireland would be underwriting  the economic performance of those banks  and i'm just saying if you want to go  your own way why would you expect voters  in those countries to do that first of  all the hard work that's been done in  every bank in the world in the last five  or six years has been to make banks  safer capital ratios are multiples of  what they were before the crisis number  one second point is when governments are  involved in bailing out financial  institutions it's a multilateral effort  the british government got involved in  bailing out the irish banks the federal  reserve reserve provided hundreds of  billions of dollars in liquidity  to uk banks like rbs and barclays  because that's where the economic assets  were deployed so bailouts in the future  should they come about and i hope they  don't and we should regulate to make  sure that that's that's increasingly  unlikely are done on a multilateral  basis based upon where the economic  assets are deployed  mr mckagan thank you very much indeed  for joining us
 

